From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 23:52:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] BD-J host: the protocol between a player and the isolated
 BD-J host

The Java code of a Blu-ray disc (BD-J) is a program the disc brings. libbluray ran it in the
player's process, kept to the disc by its own security manager, which Java 24 removed (JEP 486)
and Java 18 to 23 keep only on request: on those, libbluray 1.5's way of installing it leaves
none in force, and a disc reads the files of the player. A security manager does not keep a disc
away from the player's memory either (the JVM, its image decoders and libbluray's JNI code run
in that process). LibreELEC Hades runs BD-J in a process of its own instead, isolated by the
system, with the Java of the image; the player keeps libbluray for everything else.

This is the protocol between the two (src/libbluray/remote/bdr_protocol.h) and its framing
(bdr_wire.c): one SOCK_STREAM connection per disc, which is what a systemd socket with Accept=yes
hands to one host; messages with a header (magic, type, flags, id, length) and at most 256 KiB of
payload, 128 KiB of stream or file data; the shared memory of the ARGB planes passed with
SCM_RIGHTS, on the opening message only. Both ends are the same build of libbluray: the hello
checks the version and the layouts of the messages. Everything that comes from the host is
treated as hostile by the player: a message the framing does not allow (bad magic, a length over
the limit, a descriptor where none is expected) ends the connection, and the descriptors that
come with one are closed.
